---
import Layout from "@layouts/Layout.astro";
import Header from "@components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "@components/Footer.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "@components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import { SITE } from "@config";
---

<Layout
  title={`Adboardly Privacy Policy | ${SITE.title}`}
  description="What Adboardly accesses, what it does with it, and what it never does."
>
  <Header activeNav="projects" />
  <Breadcrumbs />
  <main id="main-content">
    <section id="privacy" class="prose mb-28 max-w-3xl">
      <h1 class="text-2xl tracking-wider sm:text-3xl">
        Adboardly Privacy Policy
      </h1>
      <p>
        <em
          >Effective date: September 15, 2026. Replaces the policy of July 13,
          2026.</em>
      </p>

      <p>
        Adboardly ("the app") is an iOS and Android application developed by
        Jesús Alfredo Hernández Alarcón that displays your Google AdMob earnings
        on your device. This policy explains what data the app accesses, what it
        does with it, and what it never does.
      </p>
      <blockquote>
        The short version: your reports go from Google to your phone and stay
        there. Adboardly has no servers and no analytics. The free version shows
        ads served by Google, and that is the only data that leaves your device.
      </blockquote>

      <h2>Google user data the app accesses</h2>
      <p>
        When you sign in with Google, the app requests the following, and
        nothing more:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Basic profile</strong> (name, email address, profile photo), used
          solely to label the account inside the app so you can tell your accounts
          apart.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>AdMob data</strong> through the official AdMob API with the read-only
          scope (<code>https://www.googleapis.com/auth/admob.readonly</code>):
          your publisher account information (publisher ID, reporting currency
          and time zone), earnings reports, and your inventory of apps and ad
          units. The scope is read-only by design; the app cannot change
          anything in your AdMob account.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How that data is used</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Reports are fetched directly from Google's servers to your device over
          HTTPS and rendered on screen for you. There is no intermediary server:
          the developer never receives, sees, stores or processes your data.
        </li>
        <li>
          The "Ask" feature interprets your questions on your device, with Apple
          Intelligence on iPhone and with Gemini Nano on Android. Your earnings
          data is never sent to any external model or service; every figure in
          every answer is computed locally from the report already on your
          device.
        </li>
        <li>
          A summary of today's and this month's earnings is stored in the app's
          private storage on your device so the home-screen widget, the Quick
          Settings tile on Android, and the Siri and on-device assistant
          integrations can display it.
        </li>
        <li>
          Notifications (the daily summary and the earnings and blocked-app
          alerts) are computed and shown locally; nothing is sent to a push
          service.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Advertising</h2>
      <p>
        The free version shows native ads served by the Google Mobile Ads SDK.
        Before any ad is requested, the app asks for your consent through
        Google's User Messaging Platform where the law requires it, and on iOS
        through Apple's App Tracking Transparency prompt. Depending on your
        choices, ads are personalized or not.
      </p>
      <p>
        To serve ads, the SDK sends Google data such as your device's IP address
        (used to estimate a coarse location), device and advertising
        identifiers, interactions with the ads, and crash and diagnostic
        information. This is described in Google's privacy policy and in the App
        Store and Google Play privacy sections of the app. Your AdMob reports
        are never part of what the SDK sends.
      </p>
      <p>
        A Premium subscription that removes the ads is planned; until it exists,
        the accounts that publish the app see no ads.
      </p>

      <h2>Storage and protection</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          On iOS, Google sign-in tokens are stored in the Keychain. On Android,
          sign-in is handled by Google Play services and the app keeps only what
          it needs in its private storage.
        </li>
        <li>
          Fetched reports are cached in the app's private storage on your
          device, so the dashboard works offline. Caches never leave the device
          and are excluded from cloud backups.
        </li>
        <li>
          You can additionally protect the app with Face ID, Touch ID or your
          device's biometrics.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What the app never does</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          No report, account or earnings data is transmitted to the developer or
          to any third party.
        </li>
        <li>
          No analytics or tracking SDKs are included beyond the advertising SDK
          described above.
        </li>
        <li>
          No data is sold, rented, or used for profiling by the developer.
        </li>
        <li>
          No human beings read your data; there is nowhere for them to read it
          from.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Limited Use disclosure</h2>
      <p>
        Adboardly's use and transfer to any other app of information received
        from Google APIs will adhere to the <a
          href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy"
          >Google API Services User Data Policy</a
        >, including the Limited Use requirements.
      </p>

      <h2>Data retention and deletion</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Signing out of an account in the app deletes its tokens and its cached
          reports and widget snapshot from the device.
        </li>
        <li>
          "Delete account" in the app does the same and also revokes the app's
          access to your Google account.
        </li>
        <li>Deleting the app removes all remaining local data.</li>
        <li>
          You can revoke Adboardly's access to your Google account at any time
          at <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/permissions"
            >myaccount.google.com/permissions</a
          >.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Children</h2>
      <p>
        The app is a professional tool for AdMob publishers and is not directed
        at children.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
      <p>
        If this policy changes, the new version will be published at this same
        address with an updated effective date.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        For any privacy question: <a href="mailto:contact@alfredohdz.io"
          >contact@alfredohdz.io</a>
      </p>

      <p class="text-sm opacity-70">
        Adboardly · Jesús Alfredo Hernández Alarcón · <a href="/adboardly/"
          >alfredohdz.io/adboardly</a
        ><br />
        Google AdMob is a trademark of Google LLC. Adboardly is an independent app
        and is not affiliated with or endorsed by Google.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
